                <pb facs="0871" n="759" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg759"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51 Contractual Obligations/34: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-07-19T15:00:00+08:00">July 19, 1935—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 19—7:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>369. Department’s 205, July 12, 5 p.m. Legation has been attempting to keep
                    itself informed regarding reported settlement of outstanding financial
                    obligations of the Chinese Government to Japanese interests. Information in this
                    regard has been conflicting.</p>
                <p>With reference to final paragraph Department’s telegram is it Department’s belief
                    that Legation should abandon discussions of American claims as a whole along
                    lines hitherto followed and advise American claimants that they should
                    individually reconsider their claims and approach Chinese Government with the
                    active assistance of the Legation for settlement on best terms available rather
                    than await some future settlement either on the basis of findings of a claims
                    commission or on the basis of continued general diplomatic pressure?</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
